# Line endings are LF everywhere: in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# The repository was already LF — 1642 of 1663 tracked files were `i/lf` before
# this file existed. What was not LF was the CHECKOUT: `core.autocrlf=true` on a
# Windows machine writes CRLF to disk, and a large number of tests here assert on
# source text (that a guard is still called, that a recipe is still applied), with
# `\n` in the expected string. `\n` cannot match `\r\n`, so six of them failed on
# every Windows checkout and passed on CI's Linux runner — for four months, a
# developer on Windows could not tell a real break from this noise.
#
# `eol=lf` is what fixes that: it pins the working-tree ending too, so a checkout
# is byte-identical on every machine and a local red is a real red. It overrides
# core.autocrlf, so nobody has to change a global git setting to work here.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are the case where a wrong ending is not cosmetic: CRLF makes the
# shebang `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which fails with a "bad interpreter" that names
# a binary that plainly exists. Covered by the rule above; stated anyway, because
# it is the one that silently breaks a deploy.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binaries, never rewritten. `text=auto` already detects these by content, but a
# heuristic that reads the first 8000 bytes is not what should stand between a
# font and corruption.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xlsx binary
